            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"UNPERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"NONE\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book defines 'life' as an existence produced by walking according to God's law. While this explains Old Testament covenantal texts (e.g., Deut 30:19), it breaks down when applied to the New Testament's absolute use of 'the life' (which the entry explicitly cites, e.g., John 14:6). Defining life as the product of law-keeping directly contradicts Paul's assertion in Galatians 3:21 that the law cannot give life. Furthermore, applying this definition uniformly fails on texts like John 5:26 (the Father having life in Himself) and 1 John 1:2 (the life pre-existing with the Father), as it would require God the Father to be a subject walking according to His own law. The counter-interpretation of 'life' as uncreated divine vitality, imparted as a gift of grace, explains both the Father's intrinsic nature and the believer's salvation without contradiction.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"'Life' (or 'the life') is covenant existence, defined strictly as the state of living produced by walking in the Way according to God's law.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [\n    \"Covenant existence — the life lived walking in the Way according to God's law.\"\n  ],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The claim that 'light' is exclusively defined as 'the law lived where men can see it'.\",\n    \"The claim that the 'tree of life' is 'the living nation, the community whose obedience is on display'.\",\n    \"The claim that the 'flaming sword' in Eden is the word of God serving to guard and preserve the way of life rather than barring it.\",\n    \"The translation of 'prosphatos' in Hebrews 10:20 as 'freshly-slain' and its application as a 'living sacrifice' paradigm for the believer's conduct.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"Life (especially 'the life' in the NT) primarily denotes eternal, divine life—the uncreated life possessed by God (John 5:26) and manifested in Christ (1 John 1:2)—which is imparted to believers as a gift of salvation by grace, not an existence produced by walking according to the law.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"'Life' (zoe) as a theological symbol primarily refers to eternal, divine vitality and unmediated fellowship with God, a reality intrinsic to God and given as a gift through Christ, transcending mere covenant obedience.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"Denies that 'life' (particularly in the NT absolute usage such as John 14:6) is merely 'covenant existence' or the resulting state of walking according to the law, as this contradicts texts that describe life as God's own uncreated existence and denies Paul's explicit assertion that the law cannot give life.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"The book defines 'life' as 'covenant existence' and asserts that 'the way produces the life' and 'Walk the pattern, and you enter the life it produces.'\",\n    \"This creates a direct theological contradiction with Galatians 3:21 ('if there had been a law given which could have given life, verily righteousness should have been by the law'), where Paul explicitly denies that walking in the law can give life.\",\n    \"The book's uniform definition also fails on John 5:26 ('For as the Father has life in himself'). Applying the book's definition here would absurdly mean that God the Father possesses 'the life lived walking in the Way according to God's law' within Himself, casting the sovereign Lawgiver as a subject acting under the covenant law.\",\n    \"Likewise, 1 John 1:1-2 speaks of 'that eternal life, which was with the Father,' indicating a pre-existent divine reality, not a state of existence generated by human covenant conduct.\",\n    \"The common interpretation recognizes that while Old Testament texts (like Deut 30:20, Lev 18:5) associate physical or covenantal blessing with obedience, the New Testament elevates 'the life' (zoe) to denote God's own eternal nature imparted to believers as a free gift, which perfectly harmonizes John 5:26, 1 John 1:2, and Galatians 3:21.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Integrates Old Testament covenant language (Deut 30, Lev 18:5) smoothly with Jesus' teaching in Matt 7:14.\",\n    \"Elegantly unifies 'the way, the truth, and the life' in John 14:6 into a single sequence of standard (truth), conduct (way), and resulting existence (life).\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Explains the pre-existent, uncreated nature of 'the life' in 1 John 1:1-2.\",\n    \"Explains John 5:26 (the Father having life in Himself) without subjecting God the Father to His own law.\",\n    \"Harmonizes with Pauline theology (Galatians 3:21, Romans 6:23), which insists that eternal life is a free gift of God in Christ, not an existence produced by the law or by human conduct.\",\n    \"Accounts for the distinction in Johannine theology between earthly existence and eternal divine life (zoe).\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [\n    \"John 5:26 (The Father having life in Himself; the Father does not possess a life defined by walking according to the law).\",\n    \"Galatians 3:21 (Paul's explicit declaration that the law cannot give life).\",\n    \"1 John 1:1-2 (The eternal life that was already with the Father from the beginning).\",\n    \"Romans 6:23 (The gift of God is eternal life, directly contrasting it with something earned or produced by human conduct).\"\n  ],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"COUNTER\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"John 5:26\",\n    \"Galatians 3:21\",\n    \"1 John 1:1-2\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
